BEGIN_HYPERDESC
<h1>Relativistic flight: the ideal drive</h1>
A delta-glider in solar orbit, far from the planets, accelerates at
10,000 g for half an hour of simulation time to half the speed of light:
its clock, speed and distance against hyperbolic motion, then its clock
coasting against 1/gamma, and the drive saved in a scenario. Another, in
lunar orbit, drives into the Moon at 10^6 g under time warp: the whole
fall within one frame, found along its path. A third passes Earth at
0.3 c, 10,000 km out: bent by post-Newtonian gravity.
END_HYPERDESC

BEGIN_ENVIRONMENT
  System Sol
  Date MJD 51982.6685767396
  Script Tests/RelFlightTest
END_ENVIRONMENT

BEGIN_FOCUS
  Ship REL
END_FOCUS

BEGIN_CAMERA
  TARGET REL
  MODE Extern
  POS 4 0 -20
  TRACKMODE TargetRelative
  FOV 50.00
END_CAMERA

BEGIN_SHIPS
REL:DeltaGlider
  STATUS Orbiting Sun
  ELEMENTS 1.5e11 0.00000 0.0 0.0 0.0 90.0 51982.6685767396
  AROT 0 0 0
END
FLY:DeltaGlider
  STATUS Orbiting Earth
  ELEMENTS 7.0e6 0.00000 0.0 0.0 0.0 0.0 51982.6685767396
  AROT 0 0 0
END
IMP:DeltaGlider
  STATUS Orbiting Moon
  ELEMENTS 1.0e7 0.00000 0.0 0.0 0.0 0.0 51982.6685767396
  AROT 0 0 0
END
END_SHIPS
